.\" Manpage for neutron.
.\" To preview locally:
.\"   man -l man/man1/neutron.1
.\" To install system-wide:
.\"   sudo install -m 0644 man/man1/neutron.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.\"   sudo mandb        # rebuild the man index
.\"
.TH NEUTRON 1 "2026-05-06" "neutron 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NAME
neutron \- low-intrusion eBPF syscall tracer for authorized Android
security assessment
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B neutron
.RB [ \-\-pid
.IR PID ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-stacks ]
.RB [ \-\-binder ]
.RB [ \-\-resolve\-paths ]
.RB [ \-\-rules
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.RI [ OTHER\ OPTIONS ]
.PP
.B neutron doctor
.PP
.B neutron window
.I CAPTURE
.B \-\-anchor
.IR SPEC
.RI [ WINDOW\ OPTIONS ]
.PP
.B neutron summarize
.I CAPTURE
.B \-\-by
.IR FIELDS
.RI [ \-\-samples\ N ]
.RI [ \-\-top\ K ]
.PP
.B neutron diff
.I BASELINE
.I TEST
.B \-\-by
.IR FIELDS
.RI [ \-\-top\ K ]
.RI [ \-\-show\-same ]
.PP
.B neutron mark
.I NAME
.RI [ \-\-phase\ start | end ]
.RI [ \-\-meta\ K=V ]
.RI [ \-\-output\ FILE ]
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B neutron
is a low-intrusion observer for Android applications.
It loads a small set of eBPF programs into the kernel via the Aya runtime,
watches the syscalls a target process makes, and either emits raw events or
runs them through a rule engine that produces high-level
.I findings
(for example "this app polled
.IR /proc/self/maps
every 2 seconds",
"this app probed
.IR /system/xbin/su
from a libc-rooted call stack",
"this process crashed with SIGSEGV holding an in-flight binder transaction
to the camera HAL",
or "this app allocated RWX memory") instead of dumping multi-megabyte raw
event logs.
.PP
The tracer does not attach with
.BR ptrace (2),
inject libraries, or modify the target's mount namespace, so it avoids many
common user-space debugger and instrumentation checks (TracerPid, loaded-library
scans, mount-table inspection).
It does
.B not
hide the presence of root, the BPF programs themselves, the kernel BPF
subsystem, or the SELinux domain of the calling process.
See the
.B DISCLOSURE SURFACE
section.
.PP
.B neutron
is intentionally
.I not
a general-purpose Android profiler (use Perfetto, systrace, simpleperf, or
Android Studio profiler for that) and not a one-click vulnerability scanner.
It is meant for security researchers, reverse engineers, RASP engineers, and
mobile platform specialists who need to understand how a protected Android
application interacts with the OS at runtime.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B doctor
Run preflight environment checks and exit.
Validates: privilege (euid 0 or
.B CAP_BPF
+
.BR CAP_SYS_ADMIN ),
architecture (must be aarch64), kernel version (\(>= 6.1), BTF availability
.RI ( /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux ),
.B tracefs
mount, BPF filesystem mount, BPF ringbuf support, the
.IR raw_syscalls/sys_enter
and
.IR raw_syscalls/sys_exit
tracepoints, the
.IR binder/binder_transaction
and
.IR binder/binder_transaction_received
tracepoints (warning if missing), the
.IR sched/sched_process_exit
tracepoint, kallsyms readability, and SELinux mode.
Each check reports
.BR PASS ", " WARN ", or " FAIL ;
the command exits non-zero if any check is
.BR FAIL .
.TP
.BI window " CAPTURE \-\-anchor SPEC " [\fB\-\-before\fR " DUR" "] [\fB\-\-after\fR " DUR" "] [...]"
Host-side post-processor that cuts windows of NDJSON events around an
anchor from a previously-captured trace file. Reads
.I CAPTURE
(or
.B -
for stdin) and writes the merged windows to standard output.
See the
.B WINDOW SUBCOMMAND
section.
.TP
.BI summarize " CAPTURE \-\-by FIELDS " [\fB\-\-samples\fR " N" "] [\fB\-\-top\fR " K" "]"
Streaming NDJSON aggregator (1.2.0). Counts events grouped by
.IR FIELDS
(comma-separated; supported keys:
.BR syscall ", " pid ", " tid ", " uid ", " comm ", " fd_path ", "
.BR ioctl_cmd ", " ioctl_name ", " ioctl_family ", " ret ", " ret_class ", "
.BR type ", " is_enter ).
Optional
.B \-\-samples N
keeps up to
.I N
raw exemplar lines per group;
.B \-\-top K
limits to the loudest
.I K
groups by count. Reads
.I CAPTURE
(or
.B -
for stdin); writes a sorted table to standard output.
.TP
.BI diff " BASELINE TEST \-\-by FIELDS " [\fB\-\-top\fR " K" "] [\fB\-\-show\-same\fR ]"
Compare two NDJSON captures aggregated on the same key (1.2.0).
Prints rows in
.IR |\(De|
descending order categorised
.BR added " / " removed " / " changed
(plus
.B same
when
.B \-\-show\-same
is set). Useful for negative-evidence workflows: "scenario A and B
both ran the camera, what specifically shifted?". Only one of
.IR BASELINE / TEST
may be
.B -
(stdin).
.TP
.BI mark " NAME " [\fB\-\-phase\fR " start" | "end" "] [\fB\-\-meta\fR " K=V" "] [\fB\-\-output\fR " FILE" "]"
Append one
.B type:"marker"
NDJSON line (1.2.0). Operators bracket external scenarios with two
.B mark
calls; downstream
.B neutron window \-\-anchor marker:\fINAME
cuts a window around the bracketed range. With
.B \-\-output FILE
the line is appended via
.B O_APPEND
(atomic on Linux for \(<= PIPE_BUF), so two concurrent writers do not
interleave; without
.B \-\-output
the line goes to standard output. See the
.B MARKER WORKFLOW
section.
.PP
When no subcommand is given,
.B neutron
runs in the default trace mode described under
.BR OPTIONS .
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Target selection
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
Trace the process whose userspace PID equals
.IR PID
(equivalent to the kernel
.BR tgid ;
all threads of the target are captured).
.IR PID =0
(the default) means trace every process \(em useful but generates large
volumes; pair with
.BR \-\-exclude\-comm .
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-children
On
.BR clone (2),
add the new child PID to the per-CPU whitelist so it is also traced.
Combine with
.B \-\-pid
.IR <parent> .
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FILE"
Write the event/finding stream to
.I FILE
instead of standard output.
The capture summary still goes to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit events as NDJSON (one JSON object per line).
This is the canonical machine-readable format and what the rule engine
consumes internally.
See the
.B OUTPUT FORMATS
section for the schema.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Emit per-event lines in addition to (or instead of) findings.
By default
.B neutron
emits only rule-engine findings.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-findings
Suppress findings.
Useful with
.B \-\-raw
to reproduce the legacy per-event behaviour of pre-rule-engine versions.
.TP
.B \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print extra diagnostic context to standard error during attach and at exit.
.SS Profiles
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Apply a named profile.
Currently only the
.B security
profile is recognised; it pre-populates
.B \-\-exclude\-comm
with a small set of high-volume Android system threads
.RI ( "RenderThread, FrameMetricsAgg, PerfStat, Profile Saver, Jit thread pool" )
that drown the trace in idle noise but never carry security-relevant signal.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-comm " LIST"
Comma-separated substring matches; any event whose
.B comm
field contains one of these is suppressed userspace-side (the BPF program
still observes it).
Example:
.B \-\-exclude\-comm
.IR "RenderThread,Jit thread pool" .
.SS Capture
.TP
.B \-\-binder
Attach the
.I binder/binder_transaction
tracepoint program in addition to
.IR raw_syscalls/sys_{enter,exit} .
Also attaches
.I binder/binder_transaction_received
when the kernel exposes it (best-effort; failure is logged but does not
abort).
Caller-side binder events use
.B nr=\-1
as a sentinel; callee-side events use
.B nr=\-4
and emit the
.B type:"binder_received"
JSON line. The two are paired by
.B debug_id
(carried in the
.B ptr_hint
wire field) to produce synthesised
.B type:"binder_call"
events with
.BR caller_pid ", " callee_pid ", " code ", " latency_us ", and " status .
See the
.B BINDER CAUSALITY
section.
.TP
.B \-\-stacks
Resolve kernel and user stacks for every event via the BPF
.B STACK_TRACES
map.
Native ELF symbols are read with
.BR goblin ;
.IR /proc/kallsyms
provides kernel frames; ART JIT regions render as
\fB<JIT>+0xN\fR.
Required for stack-aware rules (T016\(enT021).
.TP
.B \-\-resolve\-paths
On
.BR openat (2)
exit events whose in-kernel path capture failed, fall back to a
.IR /proc/<pid>/fd/<fd>
readlink to recover the path.
On
.BR connect (2),
fall back to a
.IR /proc/<pid>/net/tcp*
inode lookup.
Also enables FD-graph
.IR /proc -based
backfill (see
.BR FD\-GRAPH ).
.TP
.B \-\-alert\-rwx
Suppress every event except
.BR mmap (2)
and
.BR mprotect (2)
calls that produce an RWX or W^X region.
Useful for surfacing memory-protection anomalies in a noisy trace.
.TP
.B \-\-capture\-reads
On
.BR read (2) " / " write (2)
to a watched fd
.RI ( /proc/* " or " /sys/* ),
record the buffer pointer in the event's
.B ptr_hint
field for downstream tooling.
The buffer contents themselves are
.B not
captured.
.SS Rule engine
.TP
.BI \-\-rules " FILE"
Path to a custom YAML rule file.
Defaults to the bundled detector pack
(26 rules \(em see
.IR docs/REFERENCE.md ).
.TP
.BI \-\-findings\-drain\-interval " N"
Print drained findings every
.I N
events.
Default
.BR 256 .
Lower values surface findings sooner at the cost of more wakeups.
.TP
.BI \-\-finding\-raw\-window " N"
Per-finding
.B raw_window
length \(em number of full NDJSON lines from contributing events embedded
in each emitted finding. Default
.BR 10 .
.B 0
disables raw-line capture; the
.B aggregates
block (per-finding numerical roll-up) still populates.
.SS FD-graph poller
.TP
.BI \-\-fdgraph\-pids " POLICY"
Periodic FD-poller scope. One of:
.BR traced ,
.BR active
(default \(em explicit
.B \-\-pid
plus PIDs that produced a traced event),
.BR uid
(reserved; falls back to
.BR active
with a warning), or
.BR all
(every
.IR /proc/<NUM> ;
heavy).
.TP
.BI \-\-fdgraph\-interval " DURATION"
Poller cadence:
.BR 1s ,
.BR 500ms ,
or
.B off
to disable polling entirely. Default
.BR 1s .
.TP
.BI \-\-fdgraph\-thresholds " TIERS"
Comma-separated FD-count alert tiers (e.g.
.BR 1024,8192,90% ).
Parsed for forward-compatibility; current rules carry their own thresholds.
.TP
.BI \-\-fdgraph\-top\-paths\-n " N"
Top-N
.IR /proc/<pid>/fd/<fd>
readlink aggregation per snapshot. Default
.B 0
disables. Set to e.g.
.B 5
to populate the
.B top_paths
field on every
.B fd_snapshot
JSON line.
.SS Crash correlation
.TP
.BI \-\-lookback\-events " N"
Per-PID ring-buffer depth dumped into the
.B crash_context
field on
.B process_exit
events. Default
.BR 100 .
.B 0
disables lookback.
.TP
.BI \-\-tombstone\-dir " PATH"
Directory the tombstone watcher polls at 1 Hz. Default
.IR /data/tombstones .
Empty string disables. On hosts without the directory the watcher silently
no-ops.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-logcat
Skip spawning the
.B logcat
tail. Useful on hosts without the Android
.B logcat
binary in PATH, or when the logcat overhead is undesirable.
.SS Binder causality
.TP
.BI \-\-binder\-inflight " N"
Maximum in-flight binder transactions tracked by the userspace correlator.
Default
.BR 1024 .
.B 0
disables the correlator (raw binder / binder_received events still flow).
LRU eviction when the cap is exceeded.
.SS Capture predicates (1.2.0)
.PP
A two-stage filter. The cheap subset
.RB ( pid ", " uid ", " syscall ", " ioctl-shape ", " ret ", " latency ", and "
single-offset
.BR arg.u32@N )
lowers into BPF maps so the kernel pre-drops events before ringbuf
reservation. The remainder
.RB ( fd_path " globs, " comm " globs, " arg.u8/u16/u64 ", " binder " fields)
runs userspace-side on every event that survived the BPF stage.
The two stages share a single AND-conjunction; everything inside an
.B OR
or
.B NOT
in
.B \-\-match \fIEXPR\fR
falls through to userspace as a safe over-approximation.
.PP
Per the assessment workflow:
.BR \-\-match-fd " toggles " STATE_EMIT_REQUIRED
so kernel-side state-tracking syscalls
.RB ( open ", " close ", " dup ", " dup3 ", " socket ", " pipe2 ", "
.BR memfd_create ", " clone )
bypass the predicate and keep the userspace
.B FdGraph
consistent. Sampling and rate-limiting also bypass these so the
binder correlator never loses pair halves.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-pid " LIST"
Multi-PID match (comma-separated u32). BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-uid " LIST"
UID match (comma-separated; accepts
.IR LO..HI
ranges, capped at 1024 expanded entries). BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-syscall " LIST"
aarch64 syscall numbers (comma-separated). BPF-evaluable; reuses the
.B SYSCALL_FILTER
map.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-fd " LIST"
Glob-matched fd path (e.g.
.BR '/dev/lwis*,/dev/gxp' ).
Userspace-only; toggles
.B STATE_EMIT_REQUIRED
so kernel-side fd-state syscalls bypass the predicate.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-comm " LIST"
Glob-matched
.B comm
(e.g.
.BR 'cameraserver*,*provider' ).
Userspace-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-ioctl-cmd " LIST"
ioctl
.I cmd
32-bit word. BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-ioctl-type " LIST"
.B _IOC_TYPE
byte (e.g.
.B 0x4c
for LWIS). BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-ioctl-nr " LIST"
.B _IOC_NR
byte. BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-ioctl-dir " VALUE"
.B _IOC_DIR
=
.BR none "/" r "/" w "/" rw .
BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-ret " VALUE"
Discrete ret-class on exit events:
.BR any "/" nonzero "/" negative "/" zero .
BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-latency-min " DUR"
Minimum exit-side latency (
.BR 100us ", " 5ms ", " 2s ", or bare integer µs).
BPF-evaluable.
.TP
.B \-\-match-prot-rwx
Match
.BR mmap (2)/ mprotect (2)
events with
.BR PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC .
Userspace-only.
.TP
.B \-\-match-prot-wx
Match
.BR PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC
without
.BR PROT_READ .
Userspace-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-match-arg-u32 " 'OFF=V[,V...]'"
Typed equality / IN-set on the captured ioctl arg snapshot. Reads
.B u32
LE at byte offset
.IR OFF
of
.IR data[4..]
and matches against the value set. Single-offset BPF-evaluable
.RB ( MATCH_ARG_U32_VALS ");"
multiple offsets degrade to userspace-only.
.B \-\-match-arg-u8 ", " \-\-match-arg-u16 ", " \-\-match-arg-u64
mirror the same shape with narrower / wider widths
(userspace-only).
.TP
.BI \-\-match-binder-{code,flags,to-proc,to-thread,target-node,reply} " VALUE"
Match individual fields on
.B type:"binder"
and
.B type:"binder_call"
events. All userspace-only.
.B \-\-match-binder-target-node
accepts signed values.
.TP
.BI \-\-match " EXPR"
Boolean expression over the same field vocabulary. Grammar:
.BR AND " / " OR " / " NOT
+ parens, comparators
.BR = " / " != " / " < " / " <= " / " > " / " >= " / " IN " / " GLOB .
Top-level AND-of-atoms with BPF-evaluable fields lower into
.BR MatchSpec ;
clauses inside an
.B OR
or
.B NOT
contribute no kernel filtering. Mutually exclusive with the
individual
.BR \-\-match-* " flags."
Audit-print at startup labels each clause
.B [bpf]
or
.B [user]
so volume reduction is visible.
.SS Capture mode (1.2.0)
.TP
.BI \-\-capture " MODE"
.BR matched+context= \fIDUR\fR
arms a backward+forward window of
.IR DUR
around every match: the previous
.IR DUR
of rejected events flushes on the match, and the next
.IR DUR
emits unconditionally.
.IR DUR
accepts the same unit suffixes as
.B \-\-match-latency-min
and is capped at
.BR 30s .
.B default
or
.B matched
preserves Phase 1a/1b emit-on-match-only behaviour.
.SS Sampling (1.2.0)
.TP
.BI \-\-sample " P"
Uniform Bernoulli drop with probability
.IR 1-P .
.I P
in
.BR [0.0,\ 1.0] ;
.B 1.0
keeps everything,
.B 0.0
drops every non-exempt event. State-tracking syscalls
.RB ( open ", " close ", " dup ", " socket ", \&… ), the
.B sched_process_exit
sentinel, and binder caller / received sentinels always bypass.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate-limit " N"
Token-bucket cap of
.I N
events per second across the matched stream. Same exemption set as
.BR \-\-sample .
.SS Finding enrichment (1.2.0)
.TP
.B \-\-fd-snapshot-on-finding
When a finding fires with
.BR ioctl
evidence, read
.IR /proc/<pid>/fdinfo/<fd>
synchronously and embed as
.B fdinfo_at_event
on the JSON line. Useful for transient fds the 1-Hz fdgraph poller
misses. Best-effort: failed reads are silent.
.TP
.BI \-\-binder-services " FILE"
Path to a JSON
.RB { callee_pid : { target_node : service_name }}
map. Known
.RB ( callee_pid ", " target_node )
pairs surface a
.B service
field on
.B binder_call
events. Typically populated from
.BR "service list -p" .
.SS Build artefacts
.TP
.BI \-\-object " PATH"
Path to the compiled BPF ELF object.
Default
.IR /data/local/tmp/neutron.bpf.elf
\(em the location
.B "build.sh"
and
.B "cargo xtask deploy"
push to.
.TP
.BI \-\-pages " N"
.B Deprecated
as of 1.0.0 and silently ignored.
Kept for backward compatibility with scripts that still pass the old
PerfEventArray page count.
The kernel
.B RingBuf
size is fixed in the BPF object.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH WINDOW SUBCOMMAND
.B neutron window
cuts time- or event-bounded windows of NDJSON events around an
.I anchor
from a previously captured trace.
It is the host-side companion of the in-memory
.B crash_context
lookback that ships in
.B process_exit
events; the same intuition, but operating on the full capture file
instead of the bounded ring buffer.
.PP
.SS Anchors
One or more
.B \-\-anchor
flags. Each match becomes its own anchor; the resulting windows are merged.
Specs:
.IP \(bu
.BR finding: \fIRULE_ID\fR
\(em every
.B type:"finding"
with matching
.BR rule_id .
.IP \(bu
.B crash
\(em every
.B type:"process_exit"
with
.BR classification:"crash" .
.IP \(bu
.BR pid: \fIN\fR
\(em any event with
.B pid==N
(also matches
.B caller_pid
on
.BR binder_call ).
.IP \(bu
.BR event_id: \fIN\fR
\(em single event by the monotonic correlation token.
.IP \(bu
.BR comm: \fISUBSTRING\fR
\(em events whose
.B comm
or
.B caller_comm
contains the substring.
.IP \(bu
.BR binder_call: \fISTATUS\fR
\(em
.B type:"binder_call"
with matching
.B status
.RB ( completed ", " callee_crashed ", " unmatched ).
.IP \(bu
.BR marker: \fINAME\fR
\(em (1.2.0)
.B type:"marker"
with matching
.BR name .
Pair with
.BR "neutron mark" .
.SS Window sizing
Time-based and event-count windows are mutually exclusive \(em pick one.
.TP
.BI \-\-before " DURATION"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-\-after " DURATION"
.TP
.BI \-\-around " DURATION"
.PD
Time grammar:
.BR 5s ,
.BR 500ms ,
.BR 100us ,
.BR 1000ns .
.B \-\-around X
is shorthand for
.BR "\-\-before X \-\-after X" .
.TP
.BI \-\-before\-events " N"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-\-after\-events " N"
.TP
.BI \-\-around\-events " N"
.PD
Pure index arithmetic, ignores timestamps.
.PP
Default when no flag is supplied:
.B 100 events on each side
\(em matches the
.B \-\-lookback\-events
default.
.SS Output
Default output is NDJSON of every line in the merged windows, in the
original capture order. Overlapping or adjacent windows are merged and
deduplicated.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Print one line per merged window instead of the raw NDJSON, format:
.IR "[<from_ts>..<to_ts>] events=<N> anchors=<spec_list>" .
.SS Window examples
.PP
.EX
\&# 5-second window (\(+-) around every R003 finding.
neutron window cap.ndjson \-\-anchor finding:R003_process_crash \-\-around 5s

\&# 100 events before / 50 after every crash.
neutron window cap.ndjson \-\-anchor crash \-\-before-events 100 \-\-after-events 50

\&# Pipe windows back into the rule engine for re-analysis.
neutron window cap.ndjson \-\-anchor crash \-\-around 5s \\
  | neutron \-\-raw \-\-json \-\-rules custom-rules.yaml

\&# Summary-only of all crash + service-side windows.
neutron window cap.ndjson \\
  \-\-anchor comm:surfaceflinger \\
  \-\-anchor crash \\
  \-\-around 1s \-\-summary
.EE
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.SS Text (default)
One line per event, columns
.RI [ ts_ms ]
.IR pid / tid
.I comm
.RB ( -> | <- )
.IR syscall ( decoded\ args ).
Findings render as multi-line blocks prefixed
.BR [FINDING] .
.SS NDJSON ( \fB\-\-json\fR )
One JSON object per line. The schema covers four event kinds.
.PP
.B Syscall events
carry
.BR ts_ns ", " pid ", " tid ", " uid ", " nr ", " name ", " comm ", "
.BR phase ", " ret ", " ok ", " errno ", " "args[6]" ", " data ", " fd_kind ", "
.BR fd_path ", " rwx_alert ", " stack ", " latency_us ", " enter_ts_ns ", and "
.BR event_id .
For
.BR ioctl (2)
exits the userspace decoder may add
.BR "type:\(dqsyscall\(dq" ", " ioctl_family ", " ioctl_name ", and a typed nested object "
.RB ( dma_heap ", etc.) plus " "data_phase:\(dqexit\(dq" .
.PP
.B FD snapshot events
.RB ( "type:\(dqfd_snapshot\(dq" )
emit one per in-scope PID per
.B \-\-fdgraph\-interval
tick. Carry
.BR fd_count ", " fd_rlimit ", " fd_pct_of_rlimit ", " high_water_mark ", "
.BR growth_rate_per_sec ", and " top_paths .
Drives R001-class rules.
.PP
.B Process exit events
.RB ( "type:\(dqprocess_exit\(dq" )
fire from three independent sources \(em the BPF
.B sched_process_exit
tracepoint, the
.B logcat
tail (FATAL EXCEPTION / debuggerd / ANR), and the tombstone watcher.
Carry
.BR source ", " classification ", " exit_signal ", " signal_name ", " exit_code ", and "
.BR crash_context
(the per-PID lookback ring buffer dump). See
.BR CRASH\ CORRELATION .
.PP
.B Binder call events
.RB ( "type:\(dqbinder_call\(dq" )
are synthesised by the userspace correlator from caller-side
.B binder_transaction
and callee-side
.B binder_transaction_received
events. Carry
.BR debug_id ", " caller_pid ", " caller_uid ", " caller_comm ", " callee_pid ", "
.BR code ", " flags ", " reply ", " sent_ts_ns ", " received_ts_ns ", " latency_us ", and "
.BR status
.RB ( "completed | callee_crashed | unmatched" ).
.PP
.B Findings
.RB ( "type:\(dqfinding\(dq" )
add
.BR rule_id ", " rule_name ", " severity ", " category ", " event_count ", "
.BR first_seen_ms ", " last_seen_ms ", " period_ms ", " evidence ", " behavior ", "
.BR "interpretation[]" ", " confidence ", " false_positives ", " evidence_quality ", "
.BR capture_health ", " aggregates ", " raw_window ", and (1.2.0) " fdinfo_at_event .
The last three are populated by the engine at flush time \(em see
.BR "FINDING AGGREGATES" .
.PP
.B Marker events
.RB ( "type:\(dqmarker\(dq" ", " 1.2.0 )
are emitted by
.B neutron mark
and carry
.BR name ", optional " phase ", and optional " meta .
.PP
.B Capture-health events
.RB ( "type:\(dqcapture_health\(dq" ", " 1.2.0 )
fire once on shutdown in
.B \-\-json
mode and mirror the stderr capture-summary block. Carry
.BR events_userspace ", " events_submitted ", "
the per-cause degradation counters, and a
.B degraded:bool
flag identical to the WARNING-banner predicate.
.PP
For full schema tables see
.IR docs/REFERENCE.md " and " docs/guides/output-formats.md .
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH FINDING AGGREGATES
Every finding carries an optional
.B aggregates
sub-object computed from the contributing events.
Whichever fields apply to the matched event kinds populate; the rest are
omitted from the JSON.
.IP \(bu
.BR events_per_sec ", " min_interval_ms ", " max_interval_ms
\(em populated when at least two events matched within a non-zero span.
.IP \(bu
.B distinct_targets
\(em count of distinct
.B data
strings observed.
.IP \(bu
.BR peak_fd_count ", " peak_fd_pct_of_rlimit
\(em populated when the contributing events were
.BR fd_snapshot .
.IP \(bu
.BR distinct_callee_pids ", " distinct_binder_codes
\(em populated when the contributing events were
.BR binder_call .
.PP
The
.B raw_window
array embeds up to
.B \-\-finding\-raw\-window
(default 10) full NDJSON lines from contributing events, byte-exact, so
downstream tools can re-parse with the same parser as the live capture.
Distinct-set trackers cap at 1024 entries per finding to bound memory on
runaway rules.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH CRASH CORRELATION
Three independent sources feed the
.B type:"process_exit"
event class:
.IP \(bu
.B BPF tracepoint:
.IR sched/sched_process_exit
fires for every task termination (normal exit, fatal signal, SIGKILL,
OOM kill). The handler stamps
.B source:"tracepoint"
on the emitted line. Always available; carries no signal info because
reading
.B task_struct->exit_code
requires BTF and is deferred.
.IP \(bu
.B Logcat tail:
spawns
.B logcat -v threadtime *:F
and parses Java
.B FATAL EXCEPTION
blocks, native debuggerd
.B DEBUG
crash blocks, and
.B ANR in
lines. Stamps
.BR source:"logcat" .
.IP \(bu
.B Tombstone watcher:
polls
.IR /data/tombstones/
at 1 Hz, parses pid/signal/comm from the canonical header. Stamps
.BR source:"tombstone" .
.PP
The three sources typically all fire for a single SIGSEGV within
milliseconds. Per-process aggregation in the rule engine collapses the
fan-out;
.BR R003_process_crash
fires once per PID.
.PP
The
.B crash_context
field on each emitted line carries the last
.B \-\-lookback\-events
NDJSON lines that neutron observed for the PID before the exit, making
each finding self-contained evidence.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH BINDER CAUSALITY
With
.B \-\-binder
plus the
.I binder_transaction_received
tracepoint (auto-attached when present), the userspace correlator pairs
caller-side and callee-side binder events by the kernel-assigned
.BR debug_id .
On match it emits a synthesised
.B type:"binder_call"
NDJSON line with
.B status:"completed"
and the measured
.BR latency_us .
.PP
When a callee crashes (any
.B process_exit
with
.BR classification:"crash" ),
the correlator drains every in-flight transaction whose
.B callee_pid
matches the dying PID and emits each as
.BR status:"callee_crashed" .
This drives
.BR R004_binder_callee_crash ,
the strongest signal that a specific AIDL transaction triggered a HAL or
service crash.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DEFAULT DETECTOR PACK
26 rules ship in
.IR neutron-rules/rules/default.yaml :
T001\(enT022 (legacy anti-tamper / root-detection / recon / memory),
R001 (FD table > 90% of rlimit, FD-graph poller),
R002 (DMA-heap allocation burst, post-ioctl decoder),
R003 (process killed by fatal signal, three crash sources),
R004 (binder callee crashed mid-transaction).
A separate DexProtector RASP pack
.RB ( "DP001\(enDP008" )
is bundled at
.IR neutron-rules/rules/dexprotector-rasp.yaml .
.PP
T016\(enT021 require
.BR \-\-stacks .
R001 requires the FD-graph poller (default on, controlled by
.BR \-\-fdgraph\-pids " / " \-\-fdgraph\-interval ).
R002 requires post-exit ioctl decoding (always on for whitelisted commands).
R003 requires the
.B sched_process_exit
tracepoint (always attached) plus the lookback ring buffer
.RB ( \-\-lookback\-events )
and at least one userspace crash source for signal info.
R004 requires
.B \-\-binder
plus
.BR "\-\-binder\-inflight > 0" .
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH CAPTURE SUMMARY
On exit (or
.BR SIGINT )
.B neutron
prints a capture summary block to standard error containing the BPF
.B COUNTERS
map plus userspace counters.
Slots:
.IP
.EX
events submitted          # reached userspace
ringbuf reserve failed    # ring full \(em events DROPPED
inflight update failed    # sys_enter could not store args
inflight lookup missed    # sys_exit had no matching enter
user stack failed         # bpf_get_stackid(USER)  -> error
kernel stack failed       # bpf_get_stackid(0)     -> error
path read failed          # bpf_probe_read_user_str failed
path truncated            # path filled data[128] (no NUL)
fd lookup missed          # FD graph miss
symbolization failed      # userspace IP -> symbol failed
fd graph: N miss(es), M resolved via /proc/<pid>/fd
.EE
.IP
A
.B WARNING
banner is printed if any drop or degradation counter is non-zero, with the
text
.RB \(lq "absence of a finding is NOT conclusive" \(rq.
Treat findings derived from a degraded run as suggestive, not conclusive.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH MARKER WORKFLOW
.B neutron mark
appends a single
.B type:"marker"
NDJSON line so external scenarios bracket the live trace, and
.B neutron window \-\-anchor marker:\fINAME
cuts the bracketed window without manual timestamp grep'ing.
.PP
.EX
\&# tracer in background
neutron \-\-json \-\-output trace.ndjson &

\&# bracket the stimulus
neutron mark scenario \-\-phase start \-\-output trace.ndjson
\&./trigger\-camera\-extension\-night
neutron mark scenario \-\-phase end   \-\-output trace.ndjson

kill %1
neutron window trace.ndjson \-\-anchor marker:scenario \-\-around 5s
.EE
.PP
With
.B \-\-output FILE
the line is appended via
.B O_APPEND
\(em atomic on Linux for writes \(<= PIPE_BUF \(em so two concurrent
writers do not interleave. Without
.B \-\-output
the line goes to standard output.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH KERNEL SYMBOL RESOLUTION
With
.BR \-\-stacks ,
neutron resolves kernel-side IPs through a two-layer pipeline (1.2.0):
.IP \(bu
.B kallsyms
(/proc/kallsyms) gives full
.IB name + 0x offset
when the kernel's
.B kptr_restrict
allows reading symbol addresses.
.IP \(bu
.B /proc/modules
gives
.RB [ \fIko_name \fR]+0x offset
for IPs inside loaded modules. Not masked by
.BR kptr_restrict ,
so it stays useful when the kallsyms layer is blinded.
.PP
Bare hex stays the fallback for IPs outside any loaded module
when both layers miss.
Verbose mode logs which layers were available, e.g.
.IP
.EX
kernel resolver: 0 kallsyms + 92 module ranges \\
  (kallsyms masked; modules\-only fallback)
.EE
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH FD-GRAPH
.B neutron
maintains a userspace
.I (pid, fd) -> resource
graph so events that reference a bare fd
.RB ( ioctl ", " read ", " write ", " mmap ", " close ", " dup3 )
get enriched with
.B fd_kind
and
.B fd_path
in the JSON output.
State transitions are driven by
.BR openat ", " openat2 ", " close ", " dup ", " dup3 ", " socket ", " accept ", "
.BR accept4 ", " memfd_create ", and " exit_group .
With
.B \-\-resolve\-paths
a missing entry is backfilled via a single
.IR /proc/<pid>/fd/<fd>
readlink and the miss + backfill counts are surfaced in the capture summary.
.PP
A separate periodic poller (
.BR \-\-fdgraph\-interval ,
default
.B 1s
) reads
.IR /proc/<pid>/fd
and
.IR /proc/<pid>/limits
for in-scope PIDs and emits
.B type:"fd_snapshot"
events with the authoritative count, RLIMIT_NOFILE, high-water mark,
and growth rate. These feed
.BR R001_fd_table_exhaustion .
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Trace mode exited cleanly (operator sent SIGINT, or input EOF).
.B doctor
finished with no FAIL checks.
.B window
finished with at least one matched anchor.
.TP
.B 1
A required BPF map or program could not be loaded; an attach failed; the BPF
object did not parse;
.B doctor
recorded at least one FAIL;
.B window
hit an I/O error or invalid anchor spec.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter for the
.B env_logger
crate (e.g.
.B RUST_LOG=debug ).
Affects only userspace; BPF programs are unaffected.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /data/local/tmp/neutron.bpf.elf
Default location for the compiled BPF object.
Override with
.BR \-\-object .
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux
Required.
Aya consumes BTF for CO-RE relocation at load time.
.B doctor
verifies presence.
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/tracing/events/raw_syscalls/sys_{enter,exit}
Required tracepoints.
.B doctor
verifies presence.
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/tracing/events/binder/binder_{transaction,transaction_received}
Required only when
.B \-\-binder
is given. The
.I _received
variant is best-effort \(em a missing tracepoint is logged but does not
abort attach (the userspace correlator simply never matches and R004
stays silent).
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/tracing/events/sched/sched_process_exit
Required for crash correlation. Always attached.
.TP
.I /proc/kallsyms
Read once at start when
.B \-\-stacks
is set, to symbolize kernel frames.
A masked kallsyms (kptr_restrict \(>= 1) leaves kernel frames as raw hex.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/maps
Read per-PID for native ELF stack symbolization.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/fd/<fd>
Read on demand by the FD-graph fallback when
.B \-\-resolve\-paths
is set, and periodically by the FD-poller for in-scope PIDs.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/limits
Read by the FD-poller to extract
.BR RLIMIT_NOFILE .
.TP
.I /data/tombstones/
Polled by the tombstone watcher when
.B \-\-tombstone\-dir
is non-empty (default). Missing on hosts without Android crash reporting;
the watcher silently no-ops.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Quick start (one rooted Pixel)
.PP
.EX
# Build BPF + userspace, push to /data/local/tmp.
\&./build.sh
PID=$(adb shell pidof com.example.app)

# Run with stack-aware rules and procfs fallback path resolution.
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron \\
    --pid $PID \\
    --profile security \\
    --stacks \\
    --resolve-paths"
.EE
.SS Capture NDJSON for offline analysis
.PP
.EX
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron --pid $PID --json --binder" \\
    > app-trace.ndjson
# Cut a 5-second window around every crash:
neutron window app-trace.ndjson --anchor crash --around 5s
.EE
.SS Investigate a HAL crash triggered by a specific app
.PP
.EX
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron --pid 0 --json --binder \\
    --profile security" > capture.ndjson
# After a crash, find the binder transactions in flight when it fired:
neutron window capture.ndjson \\
    --anchor finding:R004_binder_callee_crash \\
    --around 2s
.EE
.SS Self-test on the demo target
.PP
.EX
cargo xtask demo                          # builds + pushes neutron + demo-target
adb shell su -c '/data/local/tmp/neutron --pid 0 --json' > demo-trace.ndjson &
adb shell '/data/local/tmp/demo-target'   # 18 phases, ~2s
# Ctrl-C neutron, then verify:
cargo xtask check-findings demo-trace.ndjson
.EE
.SS Verify the device is supported
.PP
.EX
adb shell su -c '/data/local/tmp/neutron doctor'
.EE
.SS Trace one app and follow its child processes
.PP
.EX
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron \\
    --pid $(adb shell pidof com.example.app) \\
    --follow-children \\
    --json"
.EE
.SS Highlight only RWX memory operations
.PP
.EX
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron --pid $PID --alert-rwx --raw"
.EE
.SS Predicate-driven LWIS capture (1.2.0)
.PP
.EX
\&# Match LWIS command-packet ioctls only; expression form.
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron --pid 0 --json \\
    --match 'syscall = 29 AND ioctl.cmd = 0xc0104c64 \\
             AND fd_path GLOB \(dq/dev/lwis*\(dq \\
             AND arg.u32@0 IN (0x10100, 0x10200, 0x20300, 0x20400)'"
.EE
.SS Summarise an existing capture
.PP
.EX
\&# Group counts by syscall + fd_path + ret_class
neutron summarize trace.ndjson --by syscall,fd_path,ret_class --top 20

\&# Compare two scenarios
neutron diff baseline.ndjson test.ndjson \\
    --by syscall,fd_path,ioctl_name --top 30
.EE
.SS Bracket a scenario with markers (1.2.0)
.PP
.EX
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron --json \\
    --output /data/local/tmp/trace.ndjson" &

adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron mark scenario \\
    --phase start --output /data/local/tmp/trace.ndjson"
adb shell './trigger-camera-night'
adb shell su -c "/data/local/tmp/neutron mark scenario \\
    --phase end --output /data/local/tmp/trace.ndjson"

kill %1
adb pull /data/local/tmp/trace.ndjson .
neutron window trace.ndjson --anchor marker:scenario --around 5s
.EE
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH WIRE FORMAT
The single source of truth is
.B SyscallEvent
in
.IR neutron-common/src/lib.rs .
v1.0 wire format is
.B 257 bytes
.RB ( "#[repr(C, packed)]" ),
verified at compile time by an assertion in both the BPF and userspace
crates.
A wire-format change requires bumping both assertions and the version note
in
.IR CHANGELOG.md .
.PP
Sprint-2 introduced three additional synthetic
.B syscall_nr
sentinels in the same wire layout (no struct bump):
.BR \-2
.RB ( fd_snapshot ),
.BR \-3
.RB ( process_exit ),
and
.BR \-4
.RB ( binder_transaction_received ).
.B \-1
remains the legacy binder caller sentinel.
.PP
Phase 1 (1.2.0) extended the BPF
.B FILTER_MAP
array from 2 to 16 slots; existing slot indices stay in place. New
slots:
.B FILTER_KEY_MATCH_BITS
(2),
.B FILTER_KEY_RET_CLASS
(3),
.B FILTER_KEY_LATENCY_MIN_US
(4),
.B FILTER_KEY_ARG_U32_OFF
(5),
.B FILTER_KEY_IOCTL_DIR
(6),
.B FILTER_KEY_STATE_EMIT_REQUIRED
(7). New BPF maps:
.BR MATCH_UID_SET ", " MATCH_IOCTL_CMD_SET ", " MATCH_IOCTL_TYPE_SET ", "
.BR MATCH_IOCTL_NR_SET ", " MATCH_ARG_U32_VALS .
The wire
.B SyscallEvent
struct itself is unchanged.
.PP
The
.B pid
and
.B tgid
field names in the wire are inverted relative to kernel terminology for
historical compatibility:
.B pid
holds the userspace PID (kernel
.BR tgid )
and is what
.B \-\-pid
matches against;
.B tgid
holds the userspace TID (kernel
.BR pid ),
distinct per thread.
The BPF source uses self-documenting local names
.RB ( userspace_pid ", " userspace_tid )
to avoid confusion.
.PP
The
.B ptr_hint
wire field carries the binder
.B debug_id
on
.B nr=\-1
and
.B nr=\-4
events so the userspace correlator can pair them by ID.
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B neutron
cannot fully infer:
.IP \(bu
Java/Kotlin method-level behaviour (no JVMTI / instrumentation; pair with
Frida).
.IP \(bu
Full Binder Parcel contents (only tracepoint metadata + the AIDL
.B code
on
.BR binder_call ;
the serialized arguments are not decoded).
.IP \(bu
Runtime behaviour fully inside userspace without syscalls (intra-process
method calls, in-process decryption of already-mapped pages).
.IP \(bu
.B exit_code
on the BPF
.B sched_process_exit
path \(em the tracepoint payload does not carry it. Userspace logcat /
tombstone sources fill in
.B exit_signal
when available.
.PP
See
.IR docs/LIMITATIONS.md
for the full list and roadmap pointers.
Per-rule false-positive scenarios are documented in
.IR docs/FALSE-POSITIVES.md .
.\" -----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DISCLOSURE SURFACE
.B neutron
does
.B not
hide:
.IP \(bu
The presence of root (KernelSU, Magisk).
.IP \(bu
Its own BPF programs (visible to
.BR "bpftool prog list" ).
.IP \(bu
Loaded BPF maps (visible to
.BR "bpftool map list" ).
.IP \(bu
The kernel BPF subsystem itself.
.IP \(bu
SELinux domain anomalies on the test bench.
.IP \(bu
Timing side-channels caused by tracepoint dispatch.
.PP
A target performing full environment fingerprinting can detect that
something is observing it.
.B neutron
is meant for authorized assessment of applications you own or have written
permission to test.
